    "cons": [
      "USB-A only, no USB-C output",
      "Less margin than a 28W panel under haze or low winter sun",
      "Sold under a few different Anker model names, which can confuse searches"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The lighter, simpler folder for hikers who mostly need to keep one phone alive.",
    "description": "Anker’s 21W folding solar charger is the lighter alternative to the BigBlue, and for a lot of backpackers it is the better carry. Anker lists two USB-A ports with its PowerIQ detection, SunPower cells, and a fold-flat fabric body with grommets for hanging it off a pack or tent. The trade is margin: at 21W rated, expect something in the high single digits to low teens of real watts in good sun, which is enough to charge a phone directly on a clear day but leaves less room when clouds pass or the sun is low. The fix is the same as with every panel on this list: feed a power bank, not the phone. A bank smooths out the dips that make a phone stop and restart charging, and it stores the midday harvest for night. If you plan to fill a 20,000mAh bank daily, step up to the 28W class; for a single phone on weekend trips, this is plenty."
  },
