    "description": "Nekteck’s 21W charger sells the same basic idea as the Anker for less money: a fold-out fabric panel, two USB-A ports, and loops for hanging it in the sun. For an outage kit or a car emergency bag that may get used twice a year, that is the right amount to spend, because the rating and the physics are the same across the class. What you give up is mostly brand depth and accessory polish, not watts. Expectations should be set the same way: a 21W panel in strong, direct sun is a single-phone charger, and in mixed weather it is a slow bank filler. Store it with a charged 10,000mAh power bank and a short cable, and you have a solar portable charger that will keep a phone alive through a multi-day outage as long as you get a few hours of decent sun. If it will ride on a pack every weekend, the Anker or BigBlue earns its premium."
