          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With a built-in cell, expect several days of direct sun to fill a 10,000mAh bank from empty, which is why manufacturers describe solar as a backup feature. With a 21W folding panel feeding the same bank, a clear summer day is usually enough. Clouds, winter sun and poor aiming can stretch either estimate considerably."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size solar panel do I need to charge a phone?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 21W folding panel is the practical minimum for charging a phone in a reasonable time, and it will usually refill a 10,000mAh bank on a good summer day. Step up to the 28W class for a 20,000mAh bank, two people, or winter use. Panels under about 10W are slow and suit only light, occasional top-ups."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I charge my phone directly from a solar panel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, in steady, direct sun. The problem is passing clouds: when the panel’s voltage drops, many phones stop charging and do not resume on their own. Running the panel into a power bank first avoids that, keeps charging steady, and lets you charge the phone at night at full speed from the bank instead."
          }
