          The small solar panel glued to the front of a power bank is too small to matter. In real sun it adds a few percent a day. If you want a phone to live off sunlight, buy a folding USB panel and a plain battery bank, and treat any built-in cell as a bonus.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Solar Phone Charging Actually Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every solar charger, whether it is a folding panel or a solar power bank charger with cells on the lid, is doing the same thing: turning sunlight into a few watts of electricity and pushing that through a USB regulator at 5 volts. The number on the box is the panel’s rating under standard lab light, which is bright, cool and perfectly aimed. Outdoors you rarely get all three at once, so real output is always lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with what a phone needs. Most current phones hold somewhere around 12-20 watt-hours in their battery. A power bank is rated in milliamp-hours at the cell voltage of about 3.7V, so a 10,000mAh bank stores roughly 37Wh and a 20,000mAh bank roughly 74Wh. After conversion losses, a 10,000mAh bank typically delivers one and a half to two full phone charges, and a 20,000mAh bank three to four. Those are the numbers solar has to replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now the sun. A useful planning unit is the peak sun hour: one hour of light at the intensity panels are rated against. A clear summer day in much of the U.S. gives four to six peak sun hours; winter and cloudy days can give one or two. Multiply panel watts by peak sun hours, then knock off a third or so for heat, angle and USB conversion, and you have a realistic daily harvest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run that math for a 21W folding panel and a decent summer day: 21W times five sun hours is 105Wh in theory, call it 50-70Wh at the USB port. That is enough to fill a 10,000mAh bank and still charge a phone. Run it for a solar power bank whose built-in cell produces one to two watts and the same day gives you perhaps 5-10Wh, before the heat of sitting in direct sun starts working against you.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap, roughly ten to one, is the whole story of this category. It is also why we split the picks between folding panels and batteries and only include one integrated solar power bank. If you want the wider picture of keeping devices powered when the grid goes down, our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> covers batteries without the solar angle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="built-in-vs-folding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Built-In Solar Cells vs a Folding Panel and a Bank
          </h2>

          <p>
            The integrated solar power bank is the product most people picture when they search for a solar portable charger, and it is the one most likely to disappoint. The appeal is obvious: one object, no cables, charge it on the dashboard and forget about it. The problem is surface area. A cell the size of a phone screen cannot collect much light, and no cell technology closes that gap. Manufacturers generally say as much in the fine print, describing solar input as an emergency or backup feature.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second, quieter problem: heat. Lithium cells do not like being hot, and the best place to put a solar power bank to charge it is in hot, direct sun, often on a car dashboard where temperatures climb far past what battery makers recommend. You end up trading battery lifespan for a trickle of charge. A folding panel avoids that, because the battery sits in the shade at the end of a cable while the panel bakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            A folding panel plus a separate bank fixes both problems. The panel can be ten to twenty times larger than a built-in cell while still folding to the size of a hardcover book, and the bank can be whatever capacity suits you. You can also replace either half independently: when the battery ages out after a few hundred cycles, the panel keeps working, and if you later buy a small power station, a good USB panel still has a job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the panel for the sun and the bank for the night.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A built-in cell is a bonus, not a charging plan. If solar is the reason you are buying, budget for a 21-28W folding panel and a 10,000-20,000mAh bank, and charge the phone from the bank.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            None of this means integrated solar banks are useless. As rugged, pocketable batteries with a flashlight and a last-ditch trickle, they are fine, and the BLAVOR in our picks is a decent one. Just buy them for the battery. And if your idea of solar charging includes laptops, CPAP machines or a small fridge, skip ahead: that is power station territory, covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a>.
          </p>
