        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing the System: Panel Watts and Battery Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size the battery first, from how many phone charges you need between sunny days. For one phone and a weekend, 10,000mAh is plenty. For two people, a week on the trail, or a multi-day outage, 20,000mAh is the better baseline. Bigger banks exist, but past about 26,800mAh you run into airline carry-on limits and the weight starts to argue for a small power station instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then size the panel to refill that battery in one decent day. Using the rough rule of half to two-thirds of the rating at the USB port, a 21W panel puts back around 50-70Wh on a strong summer day and a 28W panel proportionally more. That means a 21W panel comfortably refills a 10,000mAh bank, while a 20,000mAh bank is a better match for 28W, especially in spring and fall when the sun is lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter changes the math more than people expect. Shorter days and a lower sun angle can cut daily harvest to a third of summer’s, and a panel lying flat on the snow gets far less than one tilted toward the southern sky. If you need reliable solar charging from November to February, buy the bigger panel and invest time in aiming it; the BigBlue 28W or a Nomad 20 propped on its kickstand is the realistic floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cable choice matters too. USB-A panels push 5V, and a long, thin cable loses a surprising amount at those low voltages. Use a short, decent cable from panel to bank, and if the panel has two or three ports, do not assume they add up to more total power; most share the same panel output and simply split it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Energy Math</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
